[SUBMITTED] parsed_ok=True is_anomaly=True faulty_devices=['sshswitch', 'router17']
[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: sshswitch,router17
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The default gateway for the 10.5.0.x floor (10.5.0.254 on sshswitch, bridge br_v5) and its uplink to the core (router17) via a bonded link.

Mechanism: sshswitch and router17 are interconnected with a Linux bonding interface in round‑robin mode (mode 0). Per-packet load balancing across the two member links (sshswitch: eth6/eth7; router17: eth0/eth1) can reorder or occasionally blackhole bursts of traffic if either member experiences transient issues. Because all 10.5.0.x traffic to/from other networks transits this bond, simultaneous “freezes” are observed by multiple floor hosts when packets for their flows traverse the problematic member.

Localization and evidence:
- 10.5.0.254 resides on sshswitch br_v5 (ip addr shows 10.5.0.254/24). PC1’s ARP points to MAC 12:c4:eb:8c:87:78, which is sshswitch bond0’s MAC.
- Both sshswitch and router17 report “Bonding Mode: load balancing (round-robin)” with “MII Polling Interval (ms): 0” (no link monitoring) in /proc/net/bonding/bond0.
- The bond carries all routed traffic (OSPF adjacency sshswitch 55.5.1.2 <-> router17 55.5.1.1 is up), so any packet loss/reordering on one slave affects all clients at once.

Root cause: The interconnect between sshswitch and router17 is configured as bonding mode 0 (round‑robin) with no link-health monitoring, causing intermittent packet reordering/loss and brief network stalls for the 10.5.0.x VLAN.

Corrective fix: Change the bond on both sshswitch and router17 to a stable mode with link monitoring—preferably 802.3ad (LACP, mode 4) or active‑backup (mode 1); enable miimon 100 ms (and reasonable up/down delays). This will eliminate per-packet reordering and fail over cleanly if a member link blips. Optionally, remove unused bond0.5 from br_v5 since routing, not L2 extension, is required. 
=== END ===